#lang treason

;; Slide: "Limitations: SSE Can Misalign"
;;
;; m expects a 1, a 2, and then an expression. The use forgets the 2, so
;; treason lines the let up with the 2 and reports that it isn't a 2. The let
;; is never expanded, so there are no services on it: go-to-definition on the
;; y in its body finds nothing. treason doesn't tell missing, wrong and extra
;; subexpressions apart.

(define-syntax m
  (syntax-rules ()
    [(m 1 2 e:expr) 1]))

(m 1 (let ([y 4]) y))
